**Responses can arrive out of order, so an older, slower request can overwrite newer results. I cancel the previous request with AbortController and also ignore any response whose query no longer matches the current input. Debouncing reduces how many requests fire, but on its own it does not fix ordering.**

**The bug:**

```text
type "ip"  -> request A (slow, 600 ms)
type "iph" -> request B (fast, 150 ms)
B arrives -> show "iph" results
A arrives -> overwrites with "ip" results   <- wrong
```

**Fix 1: cancel.** Each new query aborts the previous fetch. In React, the effect cleanup is the natural place:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (!query) return;
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  fetch(`/api/suggest?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then((data: { results: Suggestion[] }) => setResults(data.results))
    .catch((e) => { if (e.name !== "AbortError") setError(true); });
  return () => ctrl.abort();
}, [query]);
```

**Fix 2: guard.** Even with abort, a response might already be resolving. Compare against the latest query (a ref or a request counter) before setting state:

```ts
const latest = useRef(0);
const id = ++latest.current;
const data = await fetchSuggestions(q);
if (id === latest.current) setResults(data);   // only the newest request wins
```

**Plus debounce** (150-300 ms) so typing "iphone" sends one or two requests instead of six. Debounce waits for a pause; throttle would send at a fixed rate while typing, which is less suitable here.

**Notes interviewers like:**
- Aborting only stops the client waiting; the server may still process the request, so debounce and caching matter for server load.
- Libraries handle this for you: TanStack Query keys requests by query and passes an `AbortSignal`; RxJS `switchMap` cancels previous inner requests.
- Handle `AbortError` silently; it is not a real error.
- The same pattern applies to any "latest wins" UI: filters, tab switching, route changes.

**A normalised cache stores each entity once, keyed by type and id, and lists or query results hold references to those ids. Updating an entity anywhere updates it everywhere it is shown, and mutations can patch one record instead of refetching every query. The cost is complexity: you need ids on everything, merge logic for lists, and garbage collection.**

**Denormalised (document) cache, as in TanStack Query by default:**

```text
["feed"]        -> [{ id: 1, author: { id: 7, name: "Asha" }, likes: 3 }, ...]
["post", 1]     -> { id: 1, author: { id: 7, name: "Asha" }, likes: 3 }
["user", 7]     -> { id: 7, name: "Asha" }
```

Liking post 1 in the modal updates `["post", 1]` but the feed still says 3 likes until it is refetched or you patch it manually.

**Normalised cache, as in Apollo, Relay, urql graphcache or RTK Query's entity adapters:**

```ts
interface EntityStore {
  Post: Record<string, { id: string; authorRef: string; likes: number }>;
  User: Record<string, { id: string; name: string }>;
}
// queries
// feed -> ["Post:1", "Post:2"]
// post(1) -> "Post:1"
```

Every view reads `Post:1`, so one write fixes them all. Apollo normalises automatically using `__typename` plus `id`; Relay requires globally unique ids.

**Writing your own (Redux Toolkit):**

```ts
const posts = createEntityAdapter<Post>();
const slice = createSlice({
  name: "posts",
  initialState: posts.getInitialState(),
  reducers: { upsertMany: posts.upsertMany, updateOne: posts.updateOne },
});
// API responses are flattened: posts with authorId, users side-loaded into their own adapter
```

**Benefits:**
- Consistency across screens without refetching.
- Cheap optimistic updates (patch one record).
- Real-time events ("post 1 now has 4 likes") apply in one place.
- Less memory for heavily shared entities (users appearing in many lists).

**Costs and gotchas:**
- **Lists are the hard part.** A new post: which cached lists should include it? A normalised cache cannot know your sort or filter semantics; you write update functions or invalidate those lists.
- Partial entities: one query fetched `{id, name}`, another `{id, name, bio}`; merging must not erase fields.
- Garbage collection: entities no query references should be evicted.
- More setup and mental overhead than a simple query cache.

**When I choose which:** simple apps or ones where screens rarely share entities, a document cache with targeted invalidation (TanStack Query) is simpler and usually enough. Apps where the same entities appear in many places and update in real time (social feeds, chat, collaboration tools, Facebook's own Relay use case) benefit from normalisation.

**IntersectionObserver tells you asynchronously when an element enters or leaves the viewport (or another root), computed by the browser off your scroll handling. Unlike scroll listeners that call getBoundingClientRect on every event, it does not force layout on the main thread for each scroll tick, so it is cheaper and simpler for lazy loading, infinite scroll and visibility tracking.**

**The scroll-listener approach and its problem:**

```ts
window.addEventListener("scroll", () => {
  const r = sentinel.getBoundingClientRect();      // can force synchronous layout
  if (r.top < window.innerHeight + 800) loadMore(); // runs dozens of times per second
});
```

You end up adding throttling, passive listeners and manual cleanup, and it is still easy to cause layout thrashing.

**With IntersectionObserver:**

```ts
const io = new IntersectionObserver(
  (entries) => {
    for (const e of entries) if (e.isIntersecting) loadMore();
  },
  { root: null, rootMargin: "0px 0px 800px 0px", threshold: 0 }
);
io.observe(sentinel);
// later: io.disconnect()
```

- **root:** the scroll container (null means the viewport).
- **rootMargin:** grows or shrinks the root box, so you can trigger before the element is actually visible (prefetch distance).
- **threshold:** the fraction of the element visible that triggers a callback, e.g. `0.5` for "half visible" or `[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]` for several steps.

**Common uses:**
- Infinite scroll sentinels.
- Lazy-loading images or components (native `loading="lazy"` covers simple images).
- Autoplaying videos only when 50% visible and pausing when they leave.
- Ad and impression tracking ("seen for 1 s at 50% visible").
- Sticky header shadows and scroll-spy navigation.

**Gotchas:**
- Callbacks are asynchronous; do not rely on them for pixel-perfect sync with scrolling.
- One observer can watch many elements; create one and reuse it rather than one per item.
- Always `unobserve` or `disconnect` on unmount to avoid leaks.
- Elements with `display: none` never intersect.
- It reports geometry, not whether the element is actually visible to the eye (occlusion or opacity); IntersectionObserver v2 adds visibility tracking in Chromium for ad-fraud use cases.

**Related observers:** ResizeObserver (element size changes) and MutationObserver (DOM changes) follow the same pattern.
